; Input-derived recursion merits review, but this structural prompt cannot
; prove that the recursive step follows attacker-controlled input or creates
; stack risk. Rust parameter nodes are not available to the `has` predicate,
; and signature parameter rows do not carry a joinable declaration identity,
; so explicit depth and budget parameters remain a known boundary.
(policy
  :schema-version 1
  :id "bifrost.correctness.rust-input-recursion-without-bound"
  :name "Input-reachable Rust recursion to review"
  :message "Review this recursive function for input-driven stack depth; this prompt does not prove stack risk"
  :severity note
  :description "A direct recursive function reachable within two call edges from a public function accepting &str or &[u8] is a review prompt, not proof that recursion advances through attacker-controlled input or creates stack risk. The Rust structural adapter does not expose parameter nodes to the `has` predicate, so explicit depth, budget, limit, fuel, remaining, and max_depth parameters cannot be excluded; bounded recursion can also match."
  :help-uri "https://bifrost.brokk.ai/static-analysis-policies/#built-in-code-smell-pack"
  :tags ["correctness" "rust" "recursion" "stack-depth"]
  :analysis
    (analysis
      :type match
      :selector
        (rql
          :schema-version 1
          (language rust
            (filter :where ((caller_id eq (field callee_id)))
              (call-sites-from :proof proven
                (union
                  (enclosing-decl
                    (function
                      :visibility public
                      :parameter-type/regex "^&(str|\\[u8\\])$"))
                  (callees :depth 2
                    (enclosing-decl
                      (function
                        :visibility public
                        :parameter-type/regex "^&(str|\\[u8\\])$"))))))))))
